// @emit-error emitProgram: nested struct fields are not supported
// A FRONTIER pin, marked at the honest current state: this program is REJECTED,
// and it should compile.
//
// The shape: an inline-object annotation whose FIELD type is a generic
// APPLICATION (`{ v: Box<i32> }`), beside any other struct with the SAME field
// NAME SET (`{ v: i32 }`).
//
// Controls, each of which compiles and runs (so the generic application is the
// only moving part):
//   `type Box = { a: i32, b: string }` + `{ v: Box }`      — nominal, not generic
//   `const o: { v: { a: i32, b: string } }`                 — inline, not generic
//   `const q: { k: i32 }` instead of `{ v: i32 }`           — no field-name collision
//   `const o: Box<i32>[]`                                   — generic, not in a field
//
// The mechanism, measured (see the program plan's D-PARSETY P2 section). Two
// halves, both necessary and together sufficient — neither alone flips this pin:
//   1. `nameFieldCode` (emit_classify.vl) codes a nested-struct field only for a
//      name `nameIsStructDecl` recognises — a `TypeDecl`'s `tdName`. A generic
//      application's `tdName` is `Box<T>`, never `Box<i32>`, so the field codes
//      -1 and `internInlineShape` DECLINES the whole outer shape: the annotation
//      mints no struct row. Its sibling `fieldTypeCode` has the missing rung:
//      `!nameIsArray(base) && structIndexOfTypeName(base) >= 0 → 15`.
//   2. `collectGenAliasShapes` (emit_collect.vl) peels only `|null` and a
//      trailing `[]` off each `TypeRef.tyName` before `gaeEnsure`, so a generic
//      application in an INTERIOR position — an inline-object field, here — is
//      never interned under its application name at all, and half 1's rung has
//      nothing to find. Adding a top-level `const w: Box<i32>` (which registers
//      the instance) plus half 1's rung, in a probe build, makes this compile.
//   … and with no row from the annotation, the outer row is minted lazily from
//      the object LITERAL by `structIndexOfObjCtx`, whose lenient scan matches by
//      field-NAME SET only — so it reuses the `{v: i32}` row and the literal is
//      built against a field coded i32. That is what turns a missing row into a
//      WRONG one, and why a `{k: i32}` sibling instead of `{v: i32}` runs.
//
// Reported by #1118 as "three generic pin shapes composed in ONE program", with
// "any TWO of the three compose fine". Both halves are refuted: two of them
// (`{v: Pair<i32,string>}` + `Box<i32|null>|null`) already fail together, and
// the failure needs no union, no nullable, no array and no generic ARGUMENT of
// any particular shape — it reproduces on `5eb115d`, before #1118.
type Box<T> = { a: T, b: string }

const o: { v: Box<i32> } = { v: { a: 1, b: "z" } }
print(o.v.b)
const q: { v: i32 } = { v: 4 }
print(q.v)
